@meta
id: 24-08
title: Registers from the command line
chapter: 24
type: lesson
skills: ex-delete, ex-copy
par: 47
minutes: 6
vscode: two tabs, select, Ctrl-X, switch, Ctrl-V, switch back, select, Ctrl-C, switch, Ctrl-V
open: order.fixture.ts refund.fixture.ts
vimrc: set hidden

@brief
`refundedOrder` is in the wrong fixture file. It belongs in
`refund.fixture.ts`, at the end, with a blank line before it. It leaves
`order.fixture.ts` entirely, blank line included.

`baseCustomer`, lines 1 to 6 including the blank line after it, is
needed in both files. Copy it to the top of `refund.fixture.ts`. It
stays where it is in `order.fixture.ts`.

`m` and `t` cannot cross a file boundary. Registers can. `d` and `y`
both take a register name as their argument: `:13,19d a` deletes the
lines into register `a`, `:1,6y b` yanks into `b`. The other end is
`pu`, put, which takes a register and an address: `:$pu a` puts after
the last line, `:0pu b` puts before the first.

@keys
| `:1,6y b` | yank lines 1 to 6 into register `b` |
| `:13,19d a` | delete lines 13 to 19 into register `a` |
| `:bn` | next buffer |
| `:$pu a` | put register `a` after the last line |
| `:0pu b` | put register `b` before the first line |
| `:pu!` | put above the current line instead of below |
| `:wqa` | write everything, quit |

@hints
Yank before you delete, so the line numbers for the yank are the ones
you can see now.
--
Both puts are linewise and take an address. `:$pu` and `:0pu` are the
two ends of the file; `:5pu a` would put after line 5.
--
`:1,6y b`, `:13,19d a`, `:bn`, `:0pu b`, `:$pu a`, `:wqa`.

@solution
    :1,6y b<Enter>  :13,19d a<Enter>  :bn<Enter>  :0pu b<Enter>  :$pu a<Enter>  :wqa<Enter>

Forty-one keystrokes, two files, no cursor movement in either.

The register is an argument, and the space before it is required
because `:d a` and `:da` are different things to the parser. The
registers are the same ones chapter 20 gave you; `"ap` would have put
`a` too. What `:pu` adds is an address. `"ap` puts wherever the cursor
is, and wherever the cursor is after `:bn` is wherever you left it,
which is never where you want the text. `:$pu a` does not care.

`:pu` is always linewise, even for a register that was yanked
characterwise. It is also the only put that takes an expression:
`:pu =range(1,5)` writes five numbered lines. Filed for later.

@start order.fixture.ts
export const baseCustomer = {
  id: 'cus_501',
  email: 'ana@example.com',
  name: 'Ana Ruiz',
};

export const pendingOrder = {
  id: 'ord_1001',
  customerId: baseCustomer.id,
  status: 'PENDING',
  total: 4200,
};

export const refundedOrder = {
  id: 'ord_1002',
  customerId: baseCustomer.id,
  status: 'CANCELLED',
  total: 1899,
};

@expected order.fixture.ts
export const baseCustomer = {
  id: 'cus_501',
  email: 'ana@example.com',
  name: 'Ana Ruiz',
};

export const pendingOrder = {
  id: 'ord_1001',
  customerId: baseCustomer.id,
  status: 'PENDING',
  total: 4200,
};

@start refund.fixture.ts
export const fullRefund = {
  id: 'ref_9001',
  orderId: 'ord_1002',
  amount: 1899,
};

@expected refund.fixture.ts
export const baseCustomer = {
  id: 'cus_501',
  email: 'ana@example.com',
  name: 'Ana Ruiz',
};

export const fullRefund = {
  id: 'ref_9001',
  orderId: 'ord_1002',
  amount: 1899,
};

export const refundedOrder = {
  id: 'ord_1002',
  customerId: baseCustomer.id,
  status: 'CANCELLED',
  total: 1899,
};
